    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. My main concern is actually with the material itself. We have quite a few bundles of loose papers and unorganized correspondence that haven't been processed yet. I'm worried that if we go with a rigid system like LCC, the lack of clear subject headings in these older, messy files might result in a lot of items being misfiled or just lost in the shuffle. How do these two systems handle high levels of initial disorder or \"unclassified\" items during the early stages of archival processing?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The Dewey Decimal System is relatively uniform and might lend itself better to quickly sorting in-tact, pre-classified documents, as it guides you through a predictable progression of subject areas and subdivisions. DDS allows you to place items within a specific numbered classification right away based on the subject matter, regardless of existing disorder, which can swiftly progress your organization.\n\n\nHowever, with the Library of Congress Classification, initial disorder is less of a concern because LCC doesn't strictly depend on the presence of predefined subject headings. Instead, LCC encourages archivists to get accustomed to subtleties and nuances of the materials. Large, unprocessed collections can be particularly well-suited to LCC; the multiple digit call numbers allow for great levels of specificity even when the catalog is incomplete. As LCC's systematics also accommodate a wide variety of subjects, it can be easier to adapt and adjust categories to emerging themes within the \"unclassified\" items, allowing for a more organic approach to organization as you gradually uncover the scope and content of your collection.",
      "source": "target",
