    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Both the Dewey Decimal System and the Library of Congress Classification have protocols to handle fragmented or highly disorganized collections, but they do so differently. DDS may initially struggle with extreme fragmentation because it relies on pre-defined ranges of subjects which may not accommodate items that don't clearly fit into already established categories. This fragmentation can be partially mitigated by placing items in the most general category when no other fit is immediate, and potentially using notes and cross-references to connect related pieces once a broader context of category is determined.\n\n\nLCC, despite its granularity, can actually handle the \"chaos\" more gracefully due to its granular nature. As you assign call numbers, you have the full range of the library's numeric and alphabetical classifications at your disposal, allowing you to leave broader categories answered where necessary and use the later codes to indicate specific subfolders or volumes. LCC's adaptability facilitates the creation of a \"map\" for the disorganized contents, giving you the ability to move pieces around logically as you uncover their connections. Furthermore, LCC includes provisions for unused or temporary numbers and provides straightforward fields for creating temporary cross-referencing, which is immensely helpful when consolidating and finalizing where items belong in chaotic collections. The granularity inherently allows for flexibility",
